.TH CHTTPSVR_REGISTER_HANDLER 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_register_handler \- register a buffered handler on the root router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_register_handler(chttpsvr srv, chttp_method_t method,
                           const char *pattern,
                           chttpsvr_handler_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Registers fn as the handler for a request that matches method and
pattern on the root router.  pattern uses segments that a forward
slash separates.  A segment of the form {name} is a named path
parameter.

A method of CHTTP_ANY registers a wildcard route.  That route matches
any of the seven real HTTP methods on the pattern that you give.  Those
methods are CHTTP_GET, CHTTP_POST, CHTTP_PUT, CHTTP_DELETE,
CHTTP_PATCH, CHTTP_HEAD and CHTTP_OPTIONS.  The handler can call
chttpsvr_req_method to get the method that really arrived.
Registration order sets the priority.  A route for one method that you
register before a CHTTP_ANY route on the same pattern wins for its own
method.  The CHTTP_ANY route then catches every other one of the seven.

A route registered for CHTTP_GET also serves HEAD requests for the
same path, as RFC 9110 section 9.3.2 needs.  The handler runs exactly
as it does for GET.  The server sends the headers of the response,
content-length included whenever the handler wrote a body, without the
body.  A route registered
explicitly for CHTTP_HEAD or for CHTTP_ANY on that same path wins over
this fallback.  This is true whatever order you register the two in.  A
path that must answer HEAD differently from GET can therefore do so.  A
path registered for neither GET nor HEAD still answers 405 to a HEAD
request.

CHTTP_ANY is a placeholder for registration time alone.  It is never
the method of a request that really arrives.  A request whose method
the server does not recognize at all never reaches any handler, and
this includes a handler that CHTTP_ANY registered.  Such a method is a
WebDAV verb such as PROPFIND, a verb such as TRACE or CONNECT, or a
custom verb.  The server rejects the request with 501 Not Implemented
before the routing runs, and even before it parses the path.  See
chttpsvr_req_method(3) for the guarantee that this gives to a handler.

pattern must start with '/'.  The library rejects a pattern that does
not start with '/' at once, with ccol_invalid_args.

The name inside a {name} segment must hold only characters from
[A-Za-z0-9_].  The library rejects a name with any other character in
it, such as a space, a hyphen or a dot, with ccol_invalid_args.  It
does this because chttpsvr_req_param can never look such a name up.
The library also rejects a pattern that uses the same {name} more than
once, for example /a/{id}/b/{id}, with ccol_invalid_args.  Nothing
could otherwise reach the value that the second one captures, because
chttpsvr_req_param always returns the FIRST name that matches.

The library decodes the values of the path segments under the path
rules of RFC 3986.  This covers a literal segment and a captured
parameter alike.  It decodes a percent-encoded sequence such as %20,
but it keeps a '+' as a literal '+'.  This matches the decoded path
that chttpsvr_req_path returns.  It differs from the decoding of a
query string, which follows application/x-www-form-urlencoded and
reads a '+' as a space.  That convention applies only to
chttpsvr_req_query and chttpsvr_req_query_one, and not to a path
parameter.

A mounted prefix owns every path under it, and the most specific mount
that owns a path handles the request alone; see
chttpsvr_subrouter(3).  The routes that this function registers sit on
the root router, which handles only a path under no mount.  A
root-level pattern therefore never answers a path under a mount, and
never skips the middleware of that mount, whatever its breadth and
whenever you registered it.  Registration order plays no part in
this.  Inside one router, the server tries the routes in registration
order.

The router scans every registered route for a match on the path and
the method.  You can register more than one method for the same path,
and every one of them is reachable, whatever the registration order.
The server answers 405 when at least one route matches the path but
none matches the method.  The 405 carries an Allow header, as RFC 9110
section 15.5.6 needs.  It lists every method that a route of the
handling router accepts for the path, in the order GET, HEAD, POST,
PUT, DELETE, PATCH, OPTIONS.  HEAD is in the list wherever a CHTTP_GET
route serves it.  It answers 404 when no route matches the path.  For
a path under a mount, the owning sub-router answers the 404 or the
405, and its middleware runs for it.  It rejects a request whose method it does not recognize at all
even earlier, with 501, before any route matching runs.  See the notes
on CHTTP_ANY above.

The match of a path also checks the percent-encoded sequences.  A
request with an invalid encoding in any path segment does not match
the route, and gives a 404.  This is true whatever methods you
registered for that pattern.  The bad segment can be a literal one,
for example /bad%ZZusers/{id} against /users/{id}.  It can also be a
captured {name} parameter, for example /users/bad%ZZvalue against
/users/{id}.  A percent-encoded sequence that decodes to a literal NUL
byte (%00) counts as an invalid encoding too.  The library does not
decode it and then compare it as it is.

You can register the same pair of method and pattern more than once.
Each call succeeds and returns ccol_success.  But the server only ever
calls the FIRST handler that you registered, under a policy where the
first one wins.

The library rejects a pattern with two slashes together, for example
/foo//bar, at registration time.  It also rejects one with a slash at
the end, for example /foo/.  The server does not treat a slash at the
end of a request path as the same thing either.  A request for
/users/42/ does not match the pattern /users/{id}.  A pattern itself
must not end in '/', so you cannot register the form with the slash at
the end as a route of its own.  Normalize such a path before it
reaches the server when the server must accept it.

The handler runs on a thread of the worker pool of the server, once the
whole body arrived; chttpsvr_req_body then gives it.  No thread waits
for a slow client in the meantime: a body that stops arriving parks
its connection with no thread, and any free worker continues it later.
The thread that runs the handler can therefore differ from the one
that began to read the body, and a handler must not assume thread
affinity across requests.  A response that meets a full socket parks in
the same way after the handler returns.  See "Slow clients" in
chttpsvr_start(3).

This function is thread-safe and you can call it at any time.  You can
also call it while another thread runs chttpsvr_destroy on the same
server.  A call that races such a destroy returns ccol_invalid_args,
and touches no freed memory.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID, or a stale handle that
                  something already destroyed; when pattern or fn is
                  NULL; when pattern does not start with '/'; when
                  pattern has two slashes together or a slash at the
                  end; when a {name} segment has a character outside
                  [A-Za-z0-9_]; or when the same {name} appears more
                  than once.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler (3),
.BR chttpsvr_use (3),
.BR chttpsvr_subrouter (3)
